From 6ce258cf1ca69d73ec04c370dd0107c8416e5bf7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viet Hung Nguyen <hvn@calif.io>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 16:33:03 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat: Optionally decode a non-UTF-8 Avro string as
 Value::Bytes

Avro specifies `string` as UTF-8, but implementations exist that never enforced
it. avro-cpp's decoder copies the bytes into a byte-oriented `std::string`
without validating (`impl/BinaryDecoder.cc`), and Java's `Utf8` holds a raw
`byte[]` that `toString` only converts lazily, so a file carrying such bytes
round-trips through both and their readers never noticed. Rejecting that input
makes such data unreadable here.

Add `util::set_non_utf8_string_as_bytes`, off by default, so nothing changes for
an existing caller unless they ask for it. With it on, a `string` whose bytes are
not valid UTF-8 decodes to `Value::Bytes` instead of failing, and `Value::Bytes`
is accepted at a `Schema::String` position in the three places that otherwise
leave the accepted value useless:

  encode_internal  encodes identically to Value::String, a length prefix then
                   the bytes, so decode-then-re-encode is byte-exact
  validate         so a decoded datum can go back to the writer that produced it
  resolve_string   invalid bytes stay Bytes; valid bytes still become a String

`Value::Bytes` rather than the bytes escaped into a `String`. Latin-1 is the
tempting escape, but applied only to invalid input it is not injective: byte
0xFF and the two bytes 0xC3 0xBF, the legitimate encoding of U+00FF, would both
yield `Value::String("\u{ff}")`, leaving re-encoding no way to know which input
to write back. Distinct variants keep that mapping injective, which is what makes
the byte-exact round trip possible.

The setting follows `set_serde_human_readable`: a `OnceLock` written by the first
caller and returning the value actually in effect, so a caller that requires it
can check rather than assume. It is read on the first `string` decode, so it has
to be set before decoding starts.

Map keys stay strict either way, since `Value::Map` is keyed by `String`. They
are not decoded independently: `decode_internal` is called recursively with
`Schema::String` and the result matched for `Value::String`, so without an
explicit arm a bad key would report `MapKeyType`, claiming the key is the wrong
type rather than bad UTF-8. Uuid reads through `Schema::Bytes` and is untouched.

No public signature changes and no new error variants. The accepting cases live
in their own test binary, as `serde_human_readable_true.rs` does, because the
setting is set-once per process; the rejecting cases stay in the unit tests. The
existing suite passes unchanged.
